# Frontend Delivery

- Follow the existing frontend framework and dependency choices; inspect package metadata before adding APIs or libraries.
- Keep business authorization and authoritative calculations on the backend.
- Use semantic HTML, keyboard-accessible controls, visible focus states, labels, and useful error announcements.
- Keep loading, empty, error, unauthorized, stale, offline, and retry states explicit for asynchronous workflows.
- Prevent duplicate submissions with client state, while relying on server idempotency for correctness.
- Do not render untrusted HTML or interpolate unescaped user/provider content into markup.
- Avoid exposing private environment variables, internal IDs, tokens, or sensitive payment data to the browser.
- Test critical flows at mobile and desktop sizes without relying only on visual snapshots.
